{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"## STOPPED — this needs a design decision, and I am not making it unilaterally\n\nPicked up in a batch selected as \"mechanical, no product judgement needed\". It is not\nmechanical, exactly as this ticket's own description warned (\"Not a mechanical fix… That is a\njudgement task about the 429 policy surface, not a find-and-replace\"). Leaving it OPEN and\nunclaimed. Below are the measured facts and the two competing options so whoever takes it can\ndecide from evidence rather than re-derive it.\n\n### Measured on current origin/main\n\n`_step0_names` sweeps 4 files. `adapters/jira/` actually holds **12** modules. The 8 unswept\nnon-`__init__` modules define **90** members in total:\n\n    acli_subprocess.py 10   adf.py 32   backend.py 25   comment_limits.py  1\n    jira_fields.py      2   outbound_fields.py 11   probe.py 5   rich_text_codec.py 4\n\nNote the description's list of newly-in-scope modules was missing two: `acli_subprocess.py`\n(which the module docstring itself names as one of the FOUR 429 behaviours — `_run_acli`'s\ndeliberate `_rate_limit_backoff` retry) and `jira_fields.py`.\n\nThe decisive number for the anti-drift variant: scanning all 8 unswept modules for members\nwhose name is already in `_CLASSIFIED_MUTATING` yields exactly **2 defs in 1 module** —\n`backend.py::set_relationship` (:217) and `backend.py::add_comment` (:271). Those are precisely\nthe two the 8a5e disposition already measured as one-line pure delegations to\n`self.transport.<same name>(...)`. Nothing else in the package collides.\n\n### Option A — derive the file set (glob `adapters/jira/*.py`)\n\nSatisfies AC1 literally and is drift-proof by construction: a new transport module is swept the\nday it lands. Cost: `test_enumeration_completeness_every_step0_name_is_accounted_for` goes RED\nagainst all 90 members, each of which must be individually classified as mutating or entered in\n`_NON_MUTATING` with a recorded reason. That classification is a real 429-policy judgement, not\nclerical — and it is the kind that fails quietly: mis-marking a member NON-MUTATING silently\nremoves it from a safety sweep, and nothing downstream catches it. It also changes what the\nsweep *is*: today it is scoped to the TRANSPORT surface, and 8a5e's disposition concluded that\nscoping is CORRECT. A glob over the package silently redefines the sweep's subject from\n\"transport\" to \"everything in the jira package\", which is a scope decision, not a refactor.\n\n### Option B — anti-drift assertion, keep the explicit list\n\nSatisfies AC1's second branch. Keeps the sweep's transport scoping (the thing 8a5e ruled\ncorrect) and fails the build when a module outside the list defines a `_CLASSIFIED_MUTATING`\nname without being a pure delegating facade. Much smaller blast radius — it is green today\nagainst 2 known-benign defs. Cost: it hinges entirely on defining \"pure delegating facade\"\nprogrammatically, and THAT is the judgement. A one-line `return self.transport.x(...)` is easy;\na delegation with a guard, a retry, a log line, or an argument transform is not, and every\nloosening of the predicate reopens the hole the assertion exists to close. It is also weaker\nthan A by design: it catches a *renamed-to-a-known-name* mutation, not a genuinely NEW mutating\nmember with a new name — which is the drift scenario the ticket's opening paragraph actually\ndescribes (\"a NEW transport module added beside those four would not be swept\").\n\n### The decision required\n\nNeither option is obviously right, and they trade off against each other rather than dominating:\nA is complete but demands 90 policy judgements and silently widens the sweep's subject;\nB is cheap and preserves the transport scoping but only guards the narrower renamed-name case.\nA third shape exists (glob restricted to a declared TRANSPORT module set, with an assertion that\nthe declaration itself stays complete) which arguably gets both properties, at the cost of a new\ndeclaration to keep honest.\n\nWhoever picks this up should choose deliberately and record the reason, since AC3's mutation\ncheck (\"a new transport module carrying an unswept mutating member turns the guard RED\") is\nsatisfied by A but NOT by B as literally worded — so the choice also settles whether AC3 needs\nrewording.\n\nNo code change made. Ticket left open and unclaimed."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1786100933668681001,"uuid":"7851109f-45fb-4ca7-828c-c02e02243c80"}